bike_sharing.csv
================

Derived from the Bike Sharing Dataset in the UCI Machine Learning Repository.

    Fanaee-T, Hadi, and Gama, Joao. "Event labeling combining ensemble
    detectors and background knowledge." Progress in Artificial Intelligence
    (2013): pp. 1-15. Springer Berlin Heidelberg.
    doi:10.1007/s13748-013-0040-3

    https://doi.org/10.24432/C5W894

The source terms ask that use in publications cite that paper, which is what
this file records.

What was changed
----------------

`scripts/build_demo_dataset.py` is the script, and it is the authority; this is
the summary.

* `dteday` and `hr` are combined into a single `rented_at` timestamp. The
  source splits them, which profiles as a datetime column whose every value is
  midnight -- the hour-of-day panel would be one bar. This is the change that
  makes the dataset worth vendoring.
* `season` and `weathersit` are decoded from small integers to labels. A bar
  chart of `1, 2, 3, 4` is a chart of nothing.
* `holiday` and `workingday` become real booleans, and `workingday` is renamed
  `working_day`; `cnt` becomes `rentals`; `atemp` becomes `feels_like`; `hum`
  becomes `humidity`.
* The four weather floats are rounded to two decimals, and the timestamps are
  written to minute precision, purely to keep the file inside a size the
  repository is willing to carry. Neither changes a distribution the report
  draws.
* `instant`, `yr`, `mnth` and `weekday` are dropped: a row number and three
  restatements of the timestamp.

Nothing is sampled, filtered or reordered. All 17,379 hourly observations are
present, in source order.

Why this dataset
----------------

Titanic, which it replaces as the example, has no datetime column and no
numeric pair correlating above 0.5 -- so the datetime card, its four temporal
panels, and both populated correlation views never appeared in the one example
anybody looks at. See #150.

Titanic remains the fixture for `tests/test_report_layout.py`, whose byte and
height ratchets are pinned to it.
